                         for this new computational capability may include optimization16,17,\n51-rate to provide a quantum speedup? Second, can we formulate a prob-                              machine learning18–21, materials science and chemistry22–24. However,\n52:lem that is hard for a classical computer but easy for a quantum com-                            realizing the full promise of quantum computing (using Shor’s algorithm\n53-puter? By computing such a benchmark task on our superconducting                                 for factoring, for example) still requires technical leaps to engineer\n54-qubit processor, we tackle both questions. Our experiment achieves                               fault-tolerant logical qubits25–29.\n55:quantum supremacy, a milestone on the path to full-scale quantum                                    To achieve quantum supremacy, we made a number of techni-\n56-computing8–14.                                                                                   cal advances which also pave the way towards error correction. We\n57-\n58-Google AI Quantum, Mountain View, CA, USA. 2Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, University of Massachusetts Amherst, Amherst, MA, USA. 3Quantum Artificial Intelligence\n--\n71-                                                                                                                               Nature | Vol 574 | 24 OCTOBER 2019 | 505\n72-\n73-Article\n74:developed fast, high-fidelity gates that can be executed simultaneously        a\n75-across a two-dimensional qubit array. We calibrated and benchmarked\n76-the processor at both the component and system level using a powerful\n77:new tool: cross-entropy benchmarking11. Finally, we used component-\n78-level fidelities to accurately predict the performance of the whole sys-\n79-tem, further showing that quantum information behaves as expected\n80-when scaling to large systems.\n81-\n82-\n83-A suitable computational task\n84:To dem